  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A woman turned to the side"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a woman. Depicts a stick-figure with breasts sitting or squatting down."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "婦人也。象形。王育說。凡女之屬皆从女。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形): a woman with breasts kneeling or standing. In the modern form, the pictogram is reversed from the Oracle Bone script form, and is \"facing\" toward the right edge of the character: the enclosed area (bounded on the right side by the downward-curving second stroke of the modern form) is the remnant of the figure's right breast, while the figure's left breast has disappeared.\nGraphically cognate to 母 (mǔ, “mother”) and 毋, which has developed similarly, but also includes dots for nipples and has retained both breasts. Compare Egyptian 𓁐.\nSimilar but unrelated to 卩, in which the person kneeling has unspecified gender.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *naq (“woman”). Compare Tibetan ཉག་མོ (nyag mo, “woman”) (Hill, 2019).\nPronunciation 2 is an exoactive derivation of pronunciation 1 (Schuessler, 2007)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "婦人也。象形。王育說。凡女之屬皆从女。",
